{
  "id": "dns-works-web-does-not",
  "name": "DNS Works, The Site Does Not",
  "category": "security",
  "difficulty": 3,
  "xp": 300,
  "level": 9,
  "description": "The name resolves, the address is right, nothing loads.",
  "brief": "🚨 SITE UNREACHABLE\n\nweb.netquest.local resolves to 10.0.2.10, which is exactly right. Ping reaches it. The web server is listening on port 80.\n\nAnd yet a connection to port 80 just hangs and gives up with nothing at all coming back. Something between the two is throwing the traffic away without a word.\n\nFind it, and let the web traffic through.",
  "hints": [
    "Test PC-01 → 10.0.2.10 on port 80 and read the Path carefully.",
    "The path stops somewhere. Which device is it?",
    "Select that device and run 'show access-list' in its terminal.",
    "Remove or invert the rule that denies TCP/80."
  ],
  "explanation": "Silence is a symptom in its own right. DNS was right, the route was right, the service was listening — but a firewall rule matched the traffic and discarded it, and discarding produces no reply, so the client could only sit and time out.\n\nThe path display is what turns that silence into information: it shows precisely how far the packet got before it vanished, which is the device worth looking at.",
